diff --git a/docs/victoriametrics/vmagent.md b/docs/victoriametrics/vmagent.md
index 87a5b27..3ea0913 100644
--- a/docs/victoriametrics/vmagent.md
+++ b/docs/victoriametrics/vmagent.md
@@ -1184,6 +1184,37 @@ These limits are approximate, so `vmagent` can underflow or overflow them by a s
 
 See also [cardinality explorer docs](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/single-server-victoriametrics/#cardinality-explorer).
 
+## Limiting the number of scrape targets per job
+
+By default, `vmagent` doesn't limit the number of active scrape targets a single job (`job_name`) can have.
+This means that a misconfigured [service discovery](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/) source
+(for example, a cloud API or catalog query that unexpectedly starts returning a huge number of targets because of a
+misconfiguration on the discovery side) can cause `vmagent` to try scraping all of the returned targets at once,
+which may spike CPU and memory usage and degrade scraping for other jobs running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+The maximum number of active scrape targets a single job is allowed to have can be limited in the following places:
+
+* Via `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag. This limit is applied by default to all the jobs
+  defined in the file pointed to by `-promscrape.config`.
+* Via `max_scrape_targets_per_job` config option at [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs) section.
+  This option overrides the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag on a per-job basis.
+  If `max_scrape_targets_per_job` is set to `0`, then the limit isn't applied to the given job,
+  even if the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag is set.
+
+The limit is applied uniformly regardless of which service discovery mechanism(s) (`static_configs`, `kubernetes_sd_configs`,
+`consul_sd_configs`, `dns_sd_configs`, etc.) supplied the targets for the job. If the number of discovered targets for a job
+exceeds the configured limit, then only the limit's worth of targets are scraped, while the rest are excluded from scraping
+instead of being scraped all at once or causing the whole job to be rejected.
+
+The excluded targets are shown as dropped targets with reason `max_scrape_targets_per_job` at the same
+`http://vmagent-host:8429/targets` and `http://vmagent-host:8429/service-discovery` [pages](#monitoring)
+which are already used for troubleshooting scrape target health, together with `http://vmagent-host:8429/api/v1/targets`.
+This makes it possible to see at a glance which job(s) are currently over the configured limit and how many targets
+are being excluded because of it.
+
+By default the limit is disabled (`0`), so existing setups aren't affected until `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob`
+or `max_scrape_targets_per_job` is explicitly configured.
+
 ## Monitoring
 
 `vmagent` exports various metrics in Prometheus exposition format at `http://vmagent-host:8429/metrics` page.
